  "question": {
    "text": "The radius of the first orbit of hydrogen is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub></math>, and the energy in the ground state is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mtext>13.6</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>. Considering a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>- particle with a mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>207</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></math> revolving around a proton as in hydrogen atom, the energy and radius of proton and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>-combination respectively in the first orbit are (assume nucleus to be stationary)"
  },
